          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Transgression is a tool in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
            to transfer
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
            classes from one
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            to another without needing a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
            between them. Rather, one has a third space with morphisms to each of the two spaces. Of course, not just
            %em any
            third space will do.
          %p
            The topological set up is as follows. We have two
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalised+cohomology+theory"} generalised cohomology theory
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi E
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E^*(-)
            %em transgress
            )
            = succeed "-classes" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi E
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E^*
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Z
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi f
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mi g
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  Z &amp;\stackrel{f}{\to}&amp; X
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow^{\mathrlap{g}}
                  \\\
                  Y
                  }
          %p
            An important example is that where we have some parameter space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            (for instance the circle
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma = S^1
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                    %mo =
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo ×
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z = \Sigma \times [\Sigma,X]
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom"} mapping space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\Sigma,X]
            of maps from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            (for instance the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+space"} loop space
            of
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\Sigma,X]
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Σ
                          %mo ×
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %mi Σ
                          %mo ,
                          %mi X
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi ev
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi p
                                  %mn 2
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %mi Σ
                          %mo ,
                          %mi X
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\Sigma \times [\Sigma, X] &amp;\stackrel{ev}{\to}&amp; X
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow^{\mathrlap{p_2}}
                  \\\
                  [\Sigma,X]
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            This setup induces
            %strong transgression to mapping spaces
            in the following.
          %p
            Now we apply the generalised cohomology theory to this diagram. As it is a cohomology theory, the arrows reverse. We have part of our route from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E^*(X)
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi E
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E^*(Y)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E^*(X)
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi E
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E^*(Z)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E^*(Y)
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E^*(Z)
            is blocked: it is a one-way street and we want to go the
            %em wrong
            way.
          %p
            However, under certain special circumstances, cohomology theories admit push-forward maps. That is, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Z
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \colon Z \to Y
            is particularly nice, there is a map
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mo !
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msup
                      %mi E
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi E
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_! \colon E^*(Z) \to E^*(Y)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_!
            (pronounced “g shriek”) has the explanation that it is a surprise that there is a map in this direction
            %sup#fnref:1>
              %a{:href => "#fn:1", :rel => "footnote"} 1
            \. Note that the push-forward map,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mo !
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msup
                      %mi E
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi E
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_! \colon E^*(Z) \to E^*(Y)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+integration"} fiber integration
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pontrjagin-Thom+collapse+map"} Pontrjagin-Thom collapse map
          %p
            Thus the key to transgression is to understand the conditions where the map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Z
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \colon Z \to Y
            admits a push-forward map. The simplest case is where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
            is a product space,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ×
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y \times W
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientable"} orientable
            for the generalised cohomology theory
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi E
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E^*(-)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            has a fundamental class in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E^*(W)
            and evaluation on this class gives a morphism
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi E
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ×
                    %mi W
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi E
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E^*(Y \times W) \to E^*(Y)
          %h2#definition Definition
          %h3#transgression_of_differential_forms Transgression of differential forms
          %p
            See at
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transgression+of+differential+forms"} transgression of differential forms
          %h3#by_pullback_and_fiber_integration By pullback and fiber integration
          %p (…)
          %h3#transgression_of_forms_through_fiber_bundles Transgression of forms through fiber bundles
          %p
            A related construction called
            %em transgression
            is the following
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \to X
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+bundle"} fiber bundle
            with typical fiber
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo :
                  %mi F
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i : F \hookrightarrow P
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ∈
                  %msubsup
                    %mi H
                    %mi dR
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\omega] \in H_{dR}^n(F)
            a class in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+cohomology"} de Rham cohomology
            of the fiber, we say this is
            %em transgressive
            if
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                there exists a form
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi cs
                      %mo ∈
                      %msup
                        %mi Ω
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} cs \in \Omega^{n}(P)
                on the total space of the bundle;
            %li
              %p
                such that restricted along
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo ↪
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \hookrightarrow P
                to the fiber it is
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∼
                        %mi ω
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim \omega
            %li
              %p
                and such that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mi cs
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d cs
                is the pull-back of a form
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi κ
                      %mo ∈
                      %msup
                        %mi Ω
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa \in \Omega^{n+1}(X)
                on the base along the bundle projection .
          %p
            See for instance section 9 of (
            %a{:href => "#Borel"}> Borel
            ).
          %p
            This construction really exhibits transgression as a special case of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connecting+homomorphism"} connecting homomorphism
            in cohomology
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  H^n(F) \to H^{n+1}(X)
          %p
            that is induced from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/short+exact+sequence"} short exact sequence
            of cocycles
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ker
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi i
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↪
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi i
                        %mo *
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  ker(i^*) \hookrightarrow \Omega^\bullet(P) \stackrel{i^*}{\to} \Omega^\bullet(F)
          %p by restricting to those elements for which it factors through
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi ker
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi i
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Omega^\bullet(X) \hookrightarrow ker(i^*)
                  \\,.
          %p
            For more on this see
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+element"} Chern-Simons element
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#transgression_in_de_rham_cohomology Transgression in de Rham Cohomology
          %p
            With a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+theory"} cohomology theory
            that has a good geometric model, such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+cohomology"} de Rham cohomology
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+integration"} integration along the fibres
          %p
            Using the notation above, with the simple case of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                    %mo =
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ×
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z = Y \times W
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                  %mo ∈
                  %msubsup
                    %mi H
                    %mi dR
                    %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↦
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi W
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mi α
                  %mo ∈
                  %msubsup
                    %mi H
                    %mi dR
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo −
                      %mi dim
                      %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\alpha \in H^k_{dR}(X) \mapsto \int_W f^* \alpha \in H^{k - \dim W}_{dR}(Y)
          %h3#transgression_of_de_rham_cohomology_to_loop_spaces Transgression of de Rham cohomology to loop spaces
          %p
            A particular application of this is in respect to the cohomology of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+spaces"} loop spaces
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/iterated+integrals"} iterated integrals
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to its
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+loop+space+object"} free loop space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ×
                  %mi L
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1 \times L X
            with evaluation as the map to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and projection to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L X
            is rather large, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibre"} fibre
            is
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1
            and it is that which needs to be controlled.
          %p
            If we worked in the realm of pure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
            (i.e. didn’t care about geometry) this transfer would be almost trivial. Indeed, if we worked with
            %em based
            loops, it would be a tautology since then the push-forward
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi Ω
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mo *
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Ω
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^*(S^1 \wedge \Omega X) \to H^{*-1}(\Omega X)
            is simply the suspension isomorphism. (We use the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smash+product"} smash product
            as we are working with
            %em based
            spaces here.)
          %p
            However, we wish to have a geometric interpretation of this, and so work in the realm of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+topology"} differential topology
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi S
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ×
                          %mi L
                          %mi M
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi M
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi L
                          %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  S^1 \times L M &amp; \to &amp; M \\
                  \\downarrow \\
                  L M
                  }
          %p
            The general lore of transgression says that this induces a map in cohomology
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi H
                    %mi dR
                    %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msubsup
                    %mi H
                    %mi dR
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi L
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^k_{dR}(M) \to H^{k-1}_{dR}(L M)
            via
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                  %mo ↦
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mi ev
                    %mo *
                  %mi α
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\alpha \mapsto \int_{S^1} ev^*\alpha.
          %p
            As with any cohomological construction, there is always the question as to whether or not it is purely cohomological or whether or not it can be lifted to whatever-it-was that defined the cohomology theory. In this case, that is
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+form"} differential form
            = succeed "s\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+vector"} tangent vector
          %p
            To reformulate transgression thus we need to understand tangent vectors on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L M
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L M
            is to identify
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mi L
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T L M
            with
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mi T
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L T M
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi γ
                  %mi L
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in T_\gamma L M
            as a loop in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T M
            with the property that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mrow
                        %mi γ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi t
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x(t) \in T_{\gamma(t)} M
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mi L
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T L M
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            tells
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma
            which direction to move in; in the other view (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mi T
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L T M
            ), each
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x(t)
            tells each
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma(t)
            which direction to move in.
          %p
            That done, we have an obvious evaluation map
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ×
                    %mi T
                    %mi L
                    %mi M
                    %mo →
                    %mi T
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1 \times T L M \to T M
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \mapsto x(t)
            and so, given a differential form on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L M
          %p
            However, that’s not all we need. There’s a very special
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+field"} vector field
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L M
            which we want to throw into the mix. This is the vector field which assigns to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi γ
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi L
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma \in L M
            the tangent vector
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi γ
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi L
                    %mi T
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma' \in L T M
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ×
                  %mi L
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1 \times L M
            then to keep the evaluation map consistent, we have to rotate the loops in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L M
            as well.
          %p
            So starting with a
            = succeed "-form," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi γ
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi L
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma \in L M
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \in S^1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo −
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k-1
            tangent vectors at
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1, \dots, x_{k-1}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            to get
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mrow
                      %mi γ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1(t), \dots, x_{k-1}(t) \in T_{\gamma(t)} M
            and also throw in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi γ
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma'(t)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            vectors at
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mrow
                      %mi γ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{\gamma(t)} M
            on which we can evaluate
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi γ
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\alpha(x_1(t),x_2(t),\dots,x_{k-1}(t),\gamma'(t))
          %p
            This is a number. Hence, by varying
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \in S^1
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1 \to \mathbb{R}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1
            yields a number:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 1
                  %mi α
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi γ
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi dt
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\int_{S^1} \alpha(x_1(t),x_2(t),\dots,x_{k-1}(t),\gamma'(t)) dt
          %p
            Thus we have a map
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mo ⨂
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi L
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigotimes^{k-1} T(L M) \to \mathbb{R}
            = succeed "-linear," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi k
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (k-1)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L M
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi L
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^{k-1}(L M)
          %p
            The reason why
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mi ev
                    %mo *
                  %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_{S^1} ev^*\alpha
            takes the form of the above formula is the following:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ev
                    %mo *
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ×
                  %mi LM
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊕
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo +
                      %mi j
                      %mo =
                      %mi k
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi LM
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi LM
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊕
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi LM
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ev^*: \Omega^k(M) \to \Omega^k(S^1\times LM)= \oplus_{i+j=k} \Omega^i(S^1) \otimes\Omega^j(LM)  = \Omega^0(S^1) \otimes \Omega^k(LM) \oplus \Omega^1(S^1)\otimes \Omega^{k-1}(LM).
          %p Thus we may write
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ev
                    %mo *
                  %mi α
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mi k
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ev^*\alpha= \alpha_0\otimes \alpha_k  + \alpha_{1}\otimes  \alpha_{k-1},
          %p
            where
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mi k
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi LM
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_k\in \Omega^k(LM)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi α
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi LM
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha^{k-1}\in \Omega^{k-1}(LM)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_1\in \Omega^1(S^1)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi α
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha^0\in \Omega^0(S^1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1
            via
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∫
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi S
                          %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_{S^1}
          #eq:int.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mi ev
                    %mo *
                  %mi α
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mo .
                  %mo .
                  %mo .
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mo .
                  %mo .
                  %mo .
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (\int_{S^1} ev^*\alpha)_{\gamma}  (x_1, ..., x_{k-1})
